<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nino Visconti</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1296">1296</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Ugolino Visconti</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nino Visconti was the last judge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallura">Gallura</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> and a leading figure in Pisan politics. Expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> in 1288 during the struggle involving his grandfather Ugolino <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/della-gherardesca">della Gherardesca</a>, he lost Gallura to the Pisan commune. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a>, his friend, placed him in the valley of princes in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purgatorio">Purgatorio</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nino-visconti.jpg" alt="Nino Visconti" width="225" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nino_Visconti.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ugolino Visconti, known as Nino, was a son of Giovanni Visconti and a grandson of Ugolino della Gherardesca. He succeeded his father as giudice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallura">Gallura</a> in 1275 or 1276 and spent most of his life moving between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> and Gallura. He was the first husband of Beatrice d'Este, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obizzo-ii-deste">Obizzo II d'Este</a>, and his emblem was a cock, a canting device on Gallura from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> gallus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His chaplain, a friar named Gomita, was caught taking bribes to release prisoners, and Nino had him hanged, an act commended for its justice; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> placed Gomita in the eighth circle of the Inferno. In 1288 Nino began to share power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> with his grandfather, but the two quarrelled. When the elder Ugolino sought the help of Archbishop Ruggieri degli Ubaldini to expel him, the archbishop exiled them both and named his own podestà and capitano del popolo.</p><p>Nino never set foot in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallura">Gallura</a> again. From exile he attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, Lucca and the Florentine Guelfs until peace was made in 1293, and he later died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>. His daughter Joanna inherited the title of Gallura and married Rizzardo IV da Camino, Count of Ceneda and Lord of Treviso. Occitan poets knew him: two anonymous coblas are addressed to him, and Terramagnino da Pisa addressed his Doctrina de Cort to him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> may have befriended him during the part of his exile Nino spent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>. In the eighth canto of the Purgatorio Dante meets him outside St Peter's gate and greets him as giudice Nin gentil. Nino asks him to remind his young daughter Giovanna to pray for him and complains that his widow Beatrice has remarried into the Milanese Visconti. Commentators have read his wife and daughter as figures of Eve and Mary, and many see misogyny in his words about Beatrice.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nino Visconti” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The last judge, <b>Nino Visconti</b>, appears as a friend of Dante in the Purgatorio.</blockquote><a href="/state/judicate-of-gallura">Judicate of Gallura</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallura">Gallura</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.72,10.4&amp;year=1286" aria-label="Pisa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4800 614 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Nino Visconti"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5250 884h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5250 884h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.72,10.4&amp;year=1286">What was here in 1286? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
